{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_361","type":"reasoning","text":"The commit was made (with node_modules symlinks? .gitignore probably excludes node_modules — good). Now I need to make the working tree dirty to see the changed files. Actually for the ownership gate to pass, the changed files after the commit must be exactly the ones owned by the spec. Currently I committed everything, so there's no diff. Let me check what the spec validate does now.\n\nActually the goal: run `pnpm harness validate --spec <spec>` and `run --dry-run` with real diff, and have changed files = my 6 files. Since everything is committed at HEAD and origin/main = HEAD, diff is empty → changes empty → ownership vacuous pass. That's not a faithful test.\n\nBetter: commit only the *base* state (without my 6 files), then leave my 6 files as uncommitted changes so the diff shows them. Let me redo: reset, remove my files temporarily, commit base+evidence-verifier.test.ts, then restore my files.\n\nSimpler: delete .git, re-init, but first stash my new files aside. Actually easiest: commit base state by temporarily moving my new files out.\n\nLet me: move the 6 new files to a temp dir, commit everything else (that's the base state), then move them back, so git diff shows exactly my 6 files as untracked. Then run validate/run without --no-diff.\n\nAlso remove node_modules junctions first to avoid committing them (though .gitignore has node_modules).\n\nLet me first remove the junctions, then do the git dance.\n\nWait — I still need node_modules for the vitest after. Actually I've already run all tests. For harness validate only node stdlib is needed (harness/src has no deps). So I can remove junctions now.\n\nLet me remove junctions, then set up git."}}
